Q6:
Map<String, Integer> map = new HashMap<>();

map.put("Aa", 1);
map.put("BB", 2);

System.out.println(map.get("Aa"));

What is true?

Options:
A) Only one value stored
B) Map size becomes 1
C) Runtime exception
D) Hash collision occurs but both entries exist

Answer: D
D) Hash collision occurs but both entries exist
Reason:
"Aa" and "BB" produce the same hashCode in Java, causing a hash collision, but HashMap still stores both entries correctly using buckets/chaining.
